{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>בִּי אֲדֹנִי</b> (Please, my lord). It is an expression of request and supplication, and in Aramaic it is בִּיָיא בִּיָּיא — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 43:20). But this is a very strange thing, to connect it to the word לְשׁוֹן טוּרְסִי (the language of Tursi), for it does not resemble it. For בִּיָיא is an entire word that does not change, and from it one would not say \"בִּי,\" and furthermore that word is not an expression of request and supplication as the Rav said, but rather it is an expression of outcry and protest over a breach and over the perversion of a matter, like the word \"אֲבוֹי\" in the sacred language. It is known in Arabic; they use it in their lamentations, all with a pataḥ under the בֵּי\"ת, and in Greek \"בְיָיא,\" the בֵּי\"ת raphe with a sheva — they say it in times of distress and pain.\n\nAnd in Bereshit Rabbah, parashat Bereshit (12:6): What is \"סֹלּוּ לָרֹכֵב בָּעֲרָבוֹת בְּיָהּ שְׁמוֹ\" (Psalms 68:5)? There is no place that does not have an official appointed over its בִּיָּיא — Agricus in one province is appointed over its בִּיָּיא, Agritus in another province is appointed over its בִּיָּיא; so who is appointed over the בִּיָּיא of the world? The Holy One, blessed be He. That is to say, in every place there is a man appointed over outcry and over perversion, and the Holy One, blessed be He, is appointed over the outcry of the oppressed in the world who cry out בִּיָּיא.\n\nAnd further before us in parashat VaYiggash Elav (Bereshit Rabbah 93:6): Judah said to him, \"בִּיָּיא you are bringing upon us,\" for thus you said to us, \"וְאָשִׂימָה עֵינִי עָלָיו\" (Genesis 44:21) — this is the setting of the eye. And in parashat VaYehi BeShallaḥ (Bereshit Rabbah 20:10): \"Perhaps I am bringing בִּיָּיא upon a creature.\" And in parashat VaYishma Yitro (Bereshit Rabbah 27:9): \"A man was appointed and took the mantle — all the burden of the community is upon him; if he saw a man bringing בִּיָּיא upon his fellow or committing a transgression and did not rebuke him, he is punished for it.\" And in parashat Ishah Ki Tazriya (see Arukh, entry ביאה): \"He cried out: Ana, בִּיָּיא upon you!\" And so in many places.\n\nAnd Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 43:20), who translated \"בִּי אֲדֹנִי\" as \"בְּבָעוּ רִבּוֹנִי\" — did not derive the word \"בִּי\" from \"בָּעוּ,\" but rather pursued the meaning, which everywhere comes in the sense of request.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra) said that \"בִּי אֲדֹנִי\" is an abbreviated form in the sacred language, like \"בִּי אֲנִי אֲדֹנִי הֶעָוֹן\" (1 Samuel 25:24), and the meaning is: do to me whatever you wish and hear me out. But if so, it would be correct for a person to say \"בִּי אָחִי\" or \"בִּי שְׁמָעֵנִי,\" yet I have not found that the word בִּי comes except with \"אֲדֹנִי\" or with the honored Name written with אָלֶ\"ף דָּלֶ\"ת, which is also an expression of lordship. Therefore I say that its meaning is: בִּי — in myself — you are lord and ruler, and two epithets came for emphasis, like \"וְלִי אֲנִי עַבְדֶּךָ\" (1 Kings 1:26), \"בִּי אֲנִי אֲדֹנִי,\" and similar to this is \"כִּי בִי בְעֶזְרֶךָ\" (Hosea 13:9) — בִּי עֶזְרְךָ, with your help; בְעֶזְרֶךָ אֲנִי.\n\nAnd it appears to me that the word בִּי is always an expression of entreating favor, as in \"בִּי אֲדֹנִי\" and \"בִּי אֲנִי אֲדֹנִי הֶעָוֹן,\" meaning: I beg you, my lord. And if so, Onkelos interpreted it well when he rendered it \"בְּבָעוּ,\" which is an expression of request in Aramaic. And Rashi's explanation that it is an expression of request and supplication is correct, but his connecting it to בִּיָּיא is not accurate, for בִּיָּיא is an expression of outcry and pain, not of request."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {0 "The final paragraph was not present in the source text provided; it has been omitted. The translation covers only the Hebrew text given. The phrase לְשׁוֹן טוּרְסִי appears to be Ramban's term for Greek (possibly related to Tyre/Tursi); rendered literally as 'the language of Tursi' per the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:38.752080330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}